      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781903383653,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to implement Token Bucket in Redis:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to implement Token Bucket in Redis:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FyImZRGrxPKq8r6q4PMs6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -308.9521594447141,
      "y": 2743.9798435238,
      "width": 967.1593017578125,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b71",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1381048107,
      "version": 99,
      "versionNonce": 1034836811,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781903383653,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why Redis works perfectly for this:\n* Speed - Sub-millisecond responses for simple operations\n* Automatic cleanup - EXPIRE removes inactive user buckets after 1 hour of no activity\n* High availability - Can be replicated across multiple Redis instances\n* Atomic operations - The MULTI/EXEC transaction ensures no race conditions between gateways",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why Redis works perfectly for this:\n* Speed - Sub-millisecond responses for simple operations\n* Automatic cleanup - EXPIRE removes inactive user buckets after 1 hour of no activity\n* High availability - Can be replicated across multiple Redis instances\n* Atomic operations - The MULTI/EXEC transaction ensures no race conditions between gateways",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "V_S4AKfWB7m00ZbY6GY1m",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -87.81058429815835,
      "y": 3356.202872153263,
      "width": 1325.59912109375,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b72",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1907509413,
      "version": 1804,
      "versionNonce": 2138051301,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781903383653,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** How to scale??\n-- Need distribute Redis load across multiple instance (instead of single redis)\n-- Need way to partition rate limit data so each request know which Redis instance to talk to\n-- Sharding consistently with clientKey (userId, IP, API key) ==> COnsistent hashing in Redis sharding, async replication\n\n** Ensure high availability and fault tolerance\nOption1: when rate limiter can't reach Redis, reject all request with 503 or 429\n==> API offline when Redis outage\nOption2: When rate limiter can't reach Redis, allow proceed all request. \n==> Keep data available, however can cause cascade failure, your server under heady load.\n\n** Minimize latency overhead\n-- API gateways should maintain pool of persistent connections instead of establish new TCP every rate limit check\n-- Geographic distribution provide biggest latency wins\n-- Can also do local caching for rate limit (risk of stale data), ==> should not mentioned\n\n** Handle \"hot keys\" -- scaling read patterns\n-- Client-side rate limiting for also read\n-- Request queuing/batching: allow client to batch multiple operations into single request, reducing the total number of rate limit checks\n-- Premium tiers: offer higher rate limits for power users. \n-- Automatic block abusive IP/API key\n-- DDos protection: detect and block malicious traffic before it reach your rate limiter (AWS shield, Cloudfare)\n\n** Handle dynamic rule configuration\n\n \n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** How to scale??\n-- Need distribute Redis load across multiple instance (instead of single redis)\n-- Need way to partition rate limit data so each request know which Redis instance to talk to\n-- Sharding consistently with clientKey (userId, IP, API key) ==> COnsistent hashing in Redis sharding, async replication\n\n** Ensure high availability and fault tolerance\nOption1: when rate limiter can't reach Redis, reject all request with 503 or 429\n==> API offline when Redis outage\nOption2: When rate limiter can't reach Redis, allow proceed all request. \n==> Keep data available, however can cause cascade failure, your server under heady load.\n\n** Minimize latency overhead\n-- API gateways should maintain pool of persistent connections instead of establish new TCP every rate limit check\n-- Geographic distribution provide biggest latency wins\n-- Can also do local caching for rate limit (risk of stale data), ==> should not mentioned\n\n** Handle \"hot keys\" -- scaling read patterns\n-- Client-side rate limiting for also read\n-- Request queuing/batching: allow client to batch multiple operations into single request, reducing the total number of rate limit checks\n-- Premium tiers: offer higher rate limits for power users. \n-- Automatic block abusive IP/API key\n-- DDos protection: detect and block malicious traffic before it reach your rate limiter (AWS shield, Cloudfare)\n\n** Handle dynamic rule configuration\n\n \n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
